## 4. Liquid Clustering vs. Partitioning vs. ZORDER

| Feature | Hive Partitioning | ZORDER | Liquid Clustering |
|---|---|---|---|
| Layout strategy | Folder-per-value | Interleaved ordering | Hilbert curve |
| Incremental support | No | No | **Yes** |
| Change keys without rewrite | No | No | **Yes** |
| Handles high cardinality | No (file explosion) | Poorly | **Yes** |
| Works with streaming | Poorly | No | **Yes** |
| Auto Optimize compatible | Partial | No | **Yes** |
| Multiple query patterns | No | Limited | **Yes** |
| Skew handling | Manual | Manual | **Automatic** |
| Small file problem | Creates them | Creates them | Merges them |

---

## 5. When to Use Liquid Clustering (Decision Guide)

Use Liquid Clustering when:
- Table is **frequently appended to** (streaming, CDC, incremental loads)
- You need to **change clustering keys** over time as query patterns evolve
- Columns have **high cardinality** (user_id, device_id, transaction_id)
- Table has **multiple query patterns** filtering on different column combinations
- You want **hands-off maintenance** — no manual OPTIMIZE scheduling needed
- Table size is **medium to large** (>10 GB; small tables don't benefit much)

Do NOT use when:
- Table is write-once / static (partitioning may be simpler and sufficient)
- You require partition-based operations like `ALTER TABLE DROP PARTITION`
- Downstream tools require explicit partition paths

