## 7. How Liquid Clustering Handles Future / Incremental Data

This is a **critical interview topic** — understand the full incremental lifecycle:

### 7.1 Write Phase
```
New data arrives (streaming / batch)
        ↓
Written as new unclustered Parquet files
        ↓
Delta log records these as "new files" with clusteringScore = 0
```

### 7.2 OPTIMIZE Phase (Incremental)
```
OPTIMIZE runs (manual or auto)
        ↓
Reads Delta log → identifies unclustered files
        ↓
Reads ONLY unclustered files into memory
        ↓
Computes Hilbert curve index for clustering columns
        ↓
Sorts data by Hilbert index
        ↓
Writes new Parquet files (optimally sized, ~128MB-1GB)
        ↓
Marks old unclustered files as "removed" in Delta log
        ↓
Updates clusteringScore in Delta log
        ↓
Already-clustered files are NOT touched
```

### 7.3 Query Phase (Data Skipping)
```
Query: SELECT * FROM sales_data WHERE customer_id = 'C123'
        ↓
Delta engine reads file-level min/max stats from Delta log
        ↓
Files where min_customer_id > 'C123' OR max_customer_id < 'C123' → SKIPPED
        ↓
Only files that MIGHT contain 'C123' are read
        ↓
Parquet row-group level filtering applies next
        ↓
Result returned
```

### 7.4 Continuous Streaming Scenario
```python
# Streaming write — works seamlessly
(spark.readStream
  .format("delta")
  .load("/bronze/events")
  .writeStream
  .format("delta")
  .option("checkpointLocation", "/checkpoints/sales")
  .trigger(availableNow=True)   # or processingTime="5 minutes"
  .toTable("sales_data")        # liquid clustered table
  .start())

# OPTIMIZE runs incrementally in background — only new files are reclustered
spark.sql("OPTIMIZE sales_data")
```

New micro-batches write unclustered files. Each `OPTIMIZE` call clusters only the new files. Over time the entire table converges to a well-clustered state.

